## Layer 1: Symbol System (core/ir/src/symbols.rs)

A DAG-modeled vocabulary of visual symbols for the entire codebase.
Lives in core/ir because it's foundational — any crate that does
display work should reference these semantic IDs rather than
hardcoding emoji or Unicode strings.

### Relationship to existing rendering infrastructure

The symbol system is a new foundational module that integrates with
the existing rendering stack:

    core/ir/src/render.rs          Renderable trait (generated file headers)
    core/ir/src/language/          Language SubDag models (comment syntax, etc.)
    core/ir/src/symbols.rs         NEW: visual vocabulary (this design)
    core/ir/src/transport/ci/      CI rendering (CiRenderer, providers)
    core/ir/src/dag.rs             to_mermaid() DAG visualization
    gunbc-dag/src/bin/dag-viz.rs   Interactive HTML visualization

How they connect:

- **Renderable trait** already handles "generated by" headers. The
  symbol system doesn't replace this — it provides the visual vocabulary
  that renderers use for status/progress output alongside generated files.

- **Language module** models format-specific rendering (comment syntax,
  file extensions, naming conventions) as SubDags. The symbol system
  follows the same pattern: semantic identifiers → concrete representations,
  modeled as composable data. A language SubDag renders code; a SymbolSet
  renders status. Both are "how to visually represent X in format Y."

- **CI providers** (GitHub Actions, GitLab CI) already emit visual
  markers — `::group::`, `::error::`, ANSI escape sequences for
  collapsible sections and annotations. The symbol system gives them a
  shared vocabulary: instead of hardcoding "✓" in GitHub and "\x1b[32m●"
  in GitLab, both use `SymbolId::Success` rendered at the appropriate
  tier.

- **dag-viz** generates interactive HTML with Mermaid flowcharts. The
  web renderer (Layer 4, future) would extend dag-viz to consume
  DagProgress JSON and render live state using symbols from the
  SymbolSet's emoji tier.

- **to_mermaid()** renders static DAG topology. The symbol system
  augments this: a "progress mermaid" mode could inject node/edge
  state colors as Mermaid style classes derived from SemanticColor.

### Compositional model (SubDag pattern)

Symbols follow the same SubDag composition pattern as the language
module. Just as `build_html_subdag()` produces a `Node<LanguageOp>`
containing `config` + `render` nodes, the symbol system models each
semantic symbol as a composable SubDag with encoding-specific nodes.

#### Encoding groups as SubDags

Each encoding tier (Emoji, Unicode, ASCII) is a SubDag — a
self-contained visual vocabulary at a specific fidelity level.
The three tiers form a fallback composition:

```
  Dag<SymbolOp> "encoding"
  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │                                              │
  │  ┌─────────┐   ┌──────────┐   ┌───────────┐ │
  │  │  emoji   │──→│ unicode  │──→│   ascii   │ │
  │  │ (richest)│   │(fallback)│   │(universal)│ │
  │  └─────────┘   └──────────┘   └───────────┘ │
  │                                              │
  └──────────────────────────────────────────────┘
```

The edges represent a fallback chain: if a renderer can't use emoji,
it falls to unicode; if not unicode, ascii. This is the same
compositional structure as the language module's config → render chain.

#### Individual symbols as SubDags

Each symbol (NodeCompleted, EdgeFlowing, etc.) is a SubDag that
composes encoding-specific atom nodes with styling metadata:

```
  Node<SymbolOp> "node_completed"
  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │  SubDag                                              │
  │                                                      │
  │  ┌────────┐   ┌────────────┐   ┌──────────────────┐ │
  │  │ config │──→│  encoding  │──→│     resolve      │ │
  │  │        │   │ (SubDag)   │   │ tier→atom select │ │
  │  └────────┘   └────────────┘   └──────────────────┘ │
  │       │                                              │
  │       │ outputs: id, color, width                    │
  │                                                      │
  │  encoding SubDag:                                    │
  │  ┌────────────────────────────────────────┐          │
  │  │ emoji:"✅"  unicode:"●"  ascii:"[x]"  │          │
  │  └────────────────────────────────────────┘          │
  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
```

This mirrors the language module exactly:
- Language SubDag: `config` node (metadata) + `render` node (operation)
- Symbol SubDag: `config` node (id, color) + `encoding` SubDag (atoms)
  + `resolve` node (tier → concrete string)

#### Composite symbols

Because symbols are SubDags, they compose naturally. A "boundary node
that completed" is built by composing the BoundaryMarker and
NodeCompleted SubDags:

```
  Node<SymbolOp> "boundary_completed"
  ┌──────────────────────────────────────┐
  │  SubDag                              │
  │                                      │
  │  ┌─────────────────┐                 │
  │  │ boundary_marker  │──┐             │
  │  │ (SubDag ref)     │  │  ┌────────┐ │
  │  └─────────────────┘  ├─→│compose │ │
  │  ┌─────────────────┐  │  └────────┘ │
  │  │ node_completed   │──┘             │
  │  │ (SubDag ref)     │               │
  │  └─────────────────┘               │
  └──────────────────────────────────────┘
```

The compose node concatenates the two symbols at the appropriate tier:
- Emoji: "🌐✅"
- Unicode: "◈●"
- ASCII: "[B][x]"

#### Animation sequences as SubDags

Spinner frames and morph sequences are also SubDags. A spinner is a
SubDag with 4 frame nodes and a cycle edge pattern:

```
  Node<SymbolOp> "spinner"
  ┌────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │  SubDag                                    │
  │                                            │
  │  ┌────────┐  ┌────────┐  ┌────────┐  ┌────────┐ │
  │  │frame_0 │→│frame_1 │→│frame_2 │→│frame_3 │ │
  │  │ 🌑/◐/| │  │ 🌒/◓// │  │ 🌓/◑/- │  │ 🌔/◒/\ │ │
  │  └────────┘  └────────┘  └────────┘  └────────┘ │
  │       ↑                                    │     │
  │       └────────────────────────────────────┘     │
  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
```

The cycle edge from frame_3 back to frame_0 makes the animation
loop. A renderer advances through the SubDag's topo order at its
frame rate.

#### SymbolOp enum

Following the language module's `LanguageOp` pattern:

```rust
/// Operations within symbol SubDags.
/// Follows the same pattern as LanguageOp for language SubDags.
#[derive(Debug, Clone, Serialize, Deserialize)]
pub enum SymbolOp {
    /// Symbol configuration: id, color, metadata.
    Config,
    /// Encoding atom: a concrete string for one tier.
    Atom { tier: Tier, value: String },
    /// Tier resolution: selects the right atom for the requested tier.
    Resolve,
    /// Composition: combines multiple symbols into one.
    Compose,
    /// Animation frame: one frame of an animated symbol.
    Frame { index: u8 },
    /// Animation cycle: loops through child frame nodes.
    Cycle,
}
```

This means `Dag<SymbolOp>` is a composable symbol definition graph,
just as `Dag<LanguageOp>` is a composable language definition graph.

#### Build functions (parallels language build_*_subdag pattern)

```rust
/// Build the NodeCompleted symbol SubDag.
/// Follows the same pattern as build_html_subdag().
pub fn build_node_completed_subdag() -> Node<SymbolOp> {
    let mut inner = Dag::new();

    inner.add_node(Node::opaque(
        "config",
        vec![],
        vec![
            Port::scalar("id", "SymbolId"),
            Port::scalar("color", "SemanticColor"),
        ],
        SymbolOp::Config,
    ));

    inner.add_node(Node::opaque(
        "emoji",
        vec![],
        vec![Port::scalar("atom", "String")],
        SymbolOp::Atom { tier: Tier::Emoji, value: "✅".into() },
    ));

    inner.add_node(Node::opaque(
        "unicode",
        vec![],
        vec![Port::scalar("atom", "String")],
        SymbolOp::Atom { tier: Tier::Unicode, value: "●".into() },
    ));

    inner.add_node(Node::opaque(
        "ascii",
        vec![],
        vec![Port::scalar("atom", "String")],
        SymbolOp::Atom { tier: Tier::Ascii, value: "[x]".into() },
    ));

    inner.add_node(Node::opaque(
        "resolve",
        vec![
            Port::scalar("tier", "Tier"),
            Port::scalar("emoji", "String"),
            Port::scalar("unicode", "String"),
            Port::scalar("ascii", "String"),
        ],
        vec![Port::scalar("rendered", "String")],
        SymbolOp::Resolve,
    ));

    // Wire encoding atoms to resolver
    inner.add_edge(Edge::new("emoji", "atom", "resolve", "emoji"));
    inner.add_edge(Edge::new("unicode", "atom", "resolve", "unicode"));
    inner.add_edge(Edge::new("ascii", "atom", "resolve", "ascii"));

    Node::subdag("node_completed", inner)
}
```

#### Why compositional symbols?

The flat `Symbol { emoji, unicode, ascii }` struct works for the
simple case. But composable SubDags enable:

1. **Composite symbols without combinatorial explosion**: boundary +
   state = 6 composites, not 6 new entries in a flat table.

2. **Animation as structure**: spinner frames and morph sequences are
   SubDags, not special-cased arrays. The executor's topo-order
   traversal naturally produces frame sequences.

3. **Custom themes via SubDag substitution**: swap an encoding atom
   node and you get a new theme. No need to copy the entire SymbolSet.

4. **Consistency with codebase philosophy**: "everything is a DAG."
   Languages are SubDags. Types are DAGs. Now symbols are SubDags.
   The same executor, topo sort, boundary detection, and testing
   infrastructure works for all of them.

5. **Static fast path**: the STANDARD SymbolSet is still a
   `&'static [Symbol]` for zero-cost lookup. The SubDag composition
   model is the *definition* layer; the static set is the *compiled*
   layer. Same pattern as how language SubDags define structure but
   `render_html_document()` is a direct function call.

### Encoding Tiers

Every symbol has three representations, from richest to most constrained:

    | Tier    | Example          | Use Case                              |
    |---------|------------------|---------------------------------------|
    | Emoji   | 🟢 ⚡ 🔴          | Modern terminals, web, Slack/Discord  |
    | Unicode | ● ◐ ✗ ═══        | Any Unicode-capable terminal          |
    | ASCII   | [x] [~] [!] ---  | Fallback, CI logs, pipes              |

Since we control the environment, the Emoji tier is the default.
Renderers pick their tier at construction time.

```rust
#[derive(Debug, Clone, Copy, PartialEq, Eq, Hash, Serialize, Deserialize)]
pub enum Tier {
    Emoji,    // Default — full color emoji
    Unicode,  // Monochrome Unicode symbols
    Ascii,    // Pure ASCII fallback
}
```

### Semantic Color

Colors are meaning-based, not raw RGB/ANSI. Renderers map these to
actual colors based on their environment (ANSI codes, CSS classes,
Mermaid style classes, CI annotation levels, etc.).

```rust
#[derive(Debug, Clone, Copy, PartialEq, Eq, Hash, Serialize, Deserialize)]
pub enum SemanticColor {
    Default,   // Neutral
    Success,   // Green — completion, "power on"
    Warning,   // Yellow — in-progress, attention
    Error,     // Red — failure, critical
    Info,      // Blue/cyan — metadata, dry-run
    Dim,       // Gray — inactive, pending, not-yet-reached
    Active,    // Bright/highlighted — currently executing
    Accent,    // Flow, energy, data movement
}
```

SemanticColor maps to different concrete representations per renderer:

    | SemanticColor | ANSI Terminal       | CSS Class        | CI Annotation  | Mermaid |
    |---------------|---------------------|------------------|----------------|---------|
    | Success       | \x1b[32m (green)    | .status-success  | ::notice::     | :::green |
    | Error         | \x1b[31m (red)      | .status-error    | ::error::      | :::red   |
    | Warning       | \x1b[33m (yellow)   | .status-warning  | ::warning::    | :::yellow|
    | Active        | \x1b[1;37m (bold)   | .status-active   | (bold text)    | :::cyan  |
    | Dim           | \x1b[2m (dim)       | .status-dim      | ::debug::      | :::gray  |
    | Accent        | \x1b[36m (cyan)     | .status-accent   | (cyan text)    | :::blue  |

### SymbolId — semantic identifiers

Code references these IDs; the actual visual representation comes from
the SymbolSet at render time. Categories mirror DAG concepts:

```rust
#[derive(Debug, Clone, Copy, PartialEq, Eq, Hash, Serialize, Deserialize)]
pub enum SymbolId {
    // === Node States ===
    NodePending,       // Not yet reached in execution order
    NodeRunning,       // Currently executing
    NodeCompleted,     // Finished successfully
    NodeFailed,        // Finished with error
    NodeSkipped,       // Guard predicate false
    NodeIntercepted,   // Dry-run mock

    // === Edge States ===
    EdgeIdle,          // No data flowed yet
    EdgeFlowing,       // Data in transit (the "power flow" moment)
    EdgeDone,          // Data delivered and consumed
    EdgeDead,          // Source was skipped, will never flow

    // === DAG Phases ===
    DagNotStarted,
    DagRunning,
    DagCompleted,
    DagFailed,

    // === Structural (graph topology) ===
    ConnectorHorizontal,  // ── or --
    ConnectorVertical,    // │ or |
    ConnectorCorner,      // └ or `
    ConnectorTee,         // ├ or |
    ConnectorCross,       // ┼ or +
    Arrow,                // → or ->
    BoundaryMarker,       // Transport/I/O boundary node

    // === Outcome (generic result indicators) ===
    Success,
    Failure,
    Warning,
    Info,

    // === Progress / Animation ===
    Spinner0,   // Animation frame 0
    Spinner1,   // Animation frame 1
    Spinner2,   // Animation frame 2
    Spinner3,   // Animation frame 3

    // === Data Summary ===
    DataList,    // Collection in output summary
    DataMap,     // Dictionary in output summary
    DataSecret,  // Redacted value
    DataUrl,     // Link
    DataTimer,   // Elapsed time
}
```

