## Layer 3: DagProgress state machine

Renderer-agnostic state that any display backend can consume.
This is the "model" — it knows nothing about terminals or HTML.

```rust
#[derive(Debug, Clone, Serialize)]
pub struct DagProgress {
    pub nodes: HashMap<NodeId, NodeProgress>,
    pub edges: HashMap<(NodeId, NodeId), EdgeProgress>,
    pub topo_order: Vec<NodeId>,
    pub phase: DagPhase,
    pub elapsed: Duration,
}

#[derive(Debug, Clone, Serialize)]
pub enum DagPhase {
    NotStarted,
    Running { current_node: NodeId },
    Completed { elapsed: Duration },
    Failed { node: NodeId, error: String },
}

#[derive(Debug, Clone, Copy, PartialEq, Eq, Serialize)]
pub enum NodeState {
    Pending,
    Running,
    Completed,
    Failed,
    Skipped,
    Intercepted,
}

#[derive(Debug, Clone, Copy, PartialEq, Eq, Serialize)]
pub enum EdgeState {
    Idle,
    Flowing,
    Done,
    Dead,
}
```

### State transitions (the "power flow")

The SimCity power-grid effect comes from edge state transitions:

    1. DAG starts:     all nodes=Pending, all edges=Idle
    2. Node A starts:  A=Running
    3. Node A done:    A=Completed, edges(A→*)=Flowing
    4. Node B starts:  B=Running, edges(*→B)=Done
    5. Node B done:    B=Completed, edges(B→*)=Flowing
    ...
    N. Last node done: DAG=Completed

The "Flowing" state is the key visual moment — it represents data
traveling along the edge. A renderer shows this using the symbol system:
  - Terminal: EdgeFlowing symbol (⚡ emoji, ═ unicode, = ascii)
  - Dynamic mode: animated cell patterns propagating along edge
  - Web: CSS animation on SVG path triggered by EdgeState::Flowing
  - CI: map to provider annotation level (::notice:: for flow)

